And I said, you can just make a binary variable

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.500
for high earners and pull them down.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.700
This is the same kind of concept.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.900
So it works over time and it also can technically work

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.740
within other groups.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.740
So is winner enough?

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:50.020
Well, you know, winner work.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.260
Maybe we need a quarter specific.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:57.260
Maybe we can add a Q4, maybe we can add a Q2 and winner.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.900
And so we go and try and add these and they don't add much.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.900
So, I mean, this is really a good fit.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.180
And this says, this is not helpful.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.860
And if we compare the two models with an AOV,

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.180
it says, oh yeah,

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.180
with these extra two degrees of freedom,

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.700
nah, it's not good enough to add anything else.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.380
So keep an eye out when you look at data

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:21.620
for easy indicator variables

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.460
that you could potentially add to the data,

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:28.220
derived from the data that can explain patterns you're seeing.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:33.820
And the next example is electric bills.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.620
As you can imagine, these are a lot,

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.860
these can be a lot more variable

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.300
'cause they're gonna be a bit more weather dependent

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.420
than ski sales, right?

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:42.860
'Cause I mean, if you decide you're taking a ski trip,

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.500
you're taking that ski trip 'cause you've booked off,

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.220
you've booked your plane flights.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.580
I mean, you're going.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.500
Electricity is much more fluid.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:51.820
Oh, we had a really cool day.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:53.620
I had to turn on the heater.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.820
Maybe it's really hot and I, or I had a party or yeah.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:02.980
So this is data from 1994 to '98.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.300
During some months,

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.420
they were paying guests staying in part of the house

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.060
and the householder wanted to estimate

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.140
the extra business-related electricity costs.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.700
So this is a really, right?

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:15.940
If you don't have a separate meter

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.740
and maybe you're running an Airbnb, this is a good idea.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.100
So what's the data look like?

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.700
You can link the data there.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.060
Let's see the bill of power.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:32.100
This is obviously 94, 29 days.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.020
Is it an estimate or not, right?

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.460
How many guests did I have that week?

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:37.860
What month is it?

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.420
What's the label of that month?

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.980
What time and what year?

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:53.420
So if I go through and just fit a model of what's my bill?

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.340
What's the days?

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.180
What's the estimate?

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.980
What's the number of guest weeks?

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:58.820
What's time?

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:00.220
So remember guest weeks here is a number.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:03.180
So, and that is appropriate in this case

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.780
because we're saying that, you know,

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.700
two guest weeks is more than three guest weeks.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.620
And what we're saying is basically proportional

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.380
and that kind of, in this case, makes some sense.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.620
And then I've got this time effect.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:16.780
And so if we look at the seasonal structure

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.620
after we adjust, so this is the residuals of this model,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:25.740
we see it's down, up, down, up, down-ish.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.180
It goes up a bit, up, down, up, down.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:35.220
When you see patterns that look like sine waves,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.940
you might have some sort of time effect

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.700
you need to model out.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.620
And so we can model that

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.740
with what we call sinusoidal terms.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:48.220
And so we do that by literally using sine and cosine.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:49.580
So we're making two here.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:50.660
Note that we are making two.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:53.460
We're doing a time minus four and a time minus five.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.820
And the only difference between these two

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:56.900
is where we shift essentially

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:00.700
where we think that peak time is.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:06.180
And so subtracting, yeah, four or five moves the peak

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.820
and both pairs repeat every 12 months

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.420
'cause it's a sine curve, right?

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:13.660
At the chosen origin,

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.780
cosine is one or minus one.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.100
Do, do, do, do, where is it?

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.140
One, yeah, origin.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:24.740
Yes, this is the four or the five?

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:25.580
Let's look.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.500
This is the four and that's the five.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.140
So that's four and that's five.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.820
And then you can see these are also shifted

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:34.060
essentially off a quarter or three months.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.860
And what does this actually do for us?

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:42.860
We end up, if we have both of these in our model,

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:47.100
we end up with this beautiful red curve

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.260
that has a peak and has a trough.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.580
And these other two essentially are added together

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.580
to make this one curve.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:02.180
We'll get into it a bit later of why we're doing this,

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:05.220
but just know that you're putting these in together

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:09.340
as a pair 'cause you're trying to explain the time,

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:10.740
the repetitive time nature.

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.780
So the combined wave,

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.620
what does it look like mathematically?

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:18.260
We've got some, oh, we call it B here,

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.660
but it is in fact a beta that you estimate

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.180
times the sine of some omega,

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:28.700
time minus some constant plus beta of cosine,

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.820
so beta of sine, beta of cosine.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:33.060
It's got the same omega of T minus C,

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.540
and this is equivalent essentially,

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:38.140
the sine plus cosine is equivalent to some factor A,

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:42.340
which is the square root of the beta coefficient squared

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:45.180
for sine plus the beta coefficient squared of cosine,

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.220
square root of that.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:50.620
So that's A sine, and we've got phi here,

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:51.780
which is some phase.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:58.180
And the W here is two pi over 12, or pi over six,

00:09:58.180 --> 00:09:59.340
and C is the origin.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.020
So we don't know where this origin is.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:02.260
A is the amplitude.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:06.220
And so we fit this with just ordinary least squares.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:08.980
So what we did earlier in that regression,

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:12.420
we just will say sine is this, cosine is this, fit it,

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.900
and they will determine once these are estimated,

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.460
essentially they'll determine what A is

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:20.580
and where the peak position is.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.660
And they are also going to determine

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.820
by this relationship where that,

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:26.660
where that,

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:32.180
let's fit that.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.940
I encourage you to fit these on your own

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:36.100
and really take a look.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:37.060
And with all these models,

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.100
you should be fitting them on your own too.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.820
So the ones we show here in the notes and in the lecture,

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:43.740
the reason we give you the data

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.260
is we want you to play with it yourself

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.540
and see what happens when you put these in.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:52.060
So the first four or the first five,

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.580
so this is the same model we had before with time.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.100
Then we had sine and cosine, sine and cosine.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.740
The only difference are this is t minus four

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:58.980
and this is t minus five.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.780
And so what you have to think here is,

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:08.780
okay, well, my sine estimate is 23 with t minus four,

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:12.660
20 with t minus five, minus 16 with cosine

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.300
and minus 2.357, right?

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:19.740
In this model, both of these terms are significant.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.260
In this model, one of them is not.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:23.620
So which is better?

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:28.580
Well, they're exactly the same.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:33.180
So what's happened is if you take the fitted values

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.980
and subtract out the fitted values from the other models,

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:39.700
so you have these two models,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.980
the only difference is the t minus four and t minus five.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:44.180
If you stick them together, the fitted values,

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:45.660
take the absolute value, subtract them,

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:46.820
and then take the maximum value,

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:48.620
the maximum difference between these two

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:53.300
is the limit of our precision of our computer, right?

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.580
I mean, there's 13 zeros in front of that.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:57.940
So what's going on?

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:59.300
How are these the same?

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:02.300
It's because to find a peak and a trough,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.380
you need to have both terms in the model.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:08.660
And how they're interacting with each other

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.980
essentially can change one or the other,

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.860
but you need to keep both terms in the model

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.340
and you need to test them together.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.260
And so that's what we'll show.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.940
The other way you could potentially do it,

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:22.700
which we don't teach, but I will mention it

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.420
because you might see it, is if you know where the peak is,

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:29.020
you can say the peak's at month four, month five,

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:32.740
you could do time minus four or five, say that's the peak,

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:37.140
and then just fit a sign term.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:40.180
And you know it, since that's the peak,

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.780
you can make it kind of work.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:44.740
The problem is if you don't know exactly where the peak is,

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:46.180
it's not determined by the data,

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.580
it's determined by you outside of the data.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.060
And these terms can't take any shape.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:54.500
So you can't just put a sign in and pray, basically,

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:56.420
that it's gonna fit.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.900
You have to put sine and cosine in

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.660
if you want the model to be able to find that,

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.900
the amplitude and the shift for you.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.300
A bit more explicitly,

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.780
since you really probably wanna see this

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:10.700
'cause the first time I saw it,

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.900
I was like, "Wait, that can't be right.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:15.820
But it is, so sine of two pi over 12 is 0.5,

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.900
cosine of two pi over 12 is 0.886.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:23.900
So 23.447 times 0.5 minus 16.276, 0.860 minus 2357,

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:31.340
28.469 times zero minus 2.357 times one is minus 2.357.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:36.420
And so this is using these coefficients

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.580
and showing you that they are, in fact, the same thing.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:45.780
So we're essentially looking at the red curve,

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.660
the weighted sum curve.

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:51.900
So when you're looking at Fourier series like this

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:55.660
in your model, you need to think it is the combination

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:56.860
of these terms that matter,

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:58.980
not individually I need to be looking at them.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:02.380
So it's very similar to if you have a factor in your model,

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:05.260
you kinda care if the individual terms are significant

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:06.380
'cause it's kinda telling you something,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.260
but to know if a factor is important,

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:09.380
we have to do the AOV test

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:12.420
and determine if the factor is important.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:16.900
And so, yeah, we use origin five from here on.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:20.420
It's not a better fit, it's a convenient choice.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:21.660
Just remember that.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:29.660
This is showing you visually what has happened,

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:34.020
kind of showing you those two curves

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:35.820
because I just showed you that they sum,

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.260
but that's kind of a mathematical thing.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:39.180
And if you wanna see it visually,

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.220
this is what's happened.

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:42.300
Time is four minus time is five.

