It'll capture most of the variability.

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:48.900
So we'll go in with four,

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:51.820
and then we just add those other pairs in,

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:54.540
plus writing solar radiation and rainfall and temperature.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:57.260
And then when we get down,

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:02.060
I just start a square, wow, 8659, that is 641.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:03.140
That's quite a big jump.

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:07.820
And once again, has to C2, both of those are significant.

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:08.980
So likely if we do the test,

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:10.780
that's gonna be significant as well.

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:13.100
Oh yeah, there we go.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:15.460
So 869 is the R square,

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:18.140
and this is the second order harmonic fit.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.340
This doesn't look like a very good fit here,

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:22.820
but remember it has to fit.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.260
It's the best fit over the whole pattern.

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:30.260
It is grabbing these weird drops,

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:35.100
which it needs to be able to mostly pick up on

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:36.780
if it's gonna fit the data well.

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:40.980
So this is actually not a bad fit,

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:43.180
and you can see it's quite a complicated fit.

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:47.980
So what does it look like in the residual space?

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:49.420
And so this was our original.

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:54.380
Yeah, as you saw, it's above and below, above and below.

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:56.820
And if I looked at this, I'd say this is not great.

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:57.700
The pattern up and down,

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:00.380
I would still say there's some seasonality to it,

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:04.420
but it's not, I'd still say there's seasonality to it.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:06.820
The second order, when we add in the weather,

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:10.780
ooh, this is, there's a seasonality,

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:12.420
but it's much, much better, right?

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:15.180
You can see how much more of the variation we're explaining

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:17.980
because we've once again shrunk it towards zero.

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:19.220
So the model's explaining more,

00:28:19.220 --> 00:28:21.860
so there's less residual variance to look at.

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:26.420
So this is, I would say overall, a much better fit.

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:29.700
So the summary of what we've done,

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:30.740
we've done a lot this week.

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:34.300
So these are, how to describe this to you guys.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:38.180
These are things you need to play with.

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:39.380
I think it's hard to learn these

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:40.780
without actually trying these models

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:43.140
and putting in different frequencies

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.780
and try a twice as fast harmonic by itself

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:47.900
and maybe half as fast and see

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:51.780
what can you actually get out of it as far as it's fit

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:53.700
and how does it affect things?

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.540
It's really good to check.

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:58.060
The bike, we're almost fully done with the bike.

00:28:58.060 --> 00:29:00.500
We still have a bit left,

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:03.940
but basically we're done with the bike

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:07.420
as far as adding things to its mean structure.

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:08.980
So we're not gonna get more complicated with the bike.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:11.020
We've added these curves for time,

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:12.420
we've added weather effects,

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:17.300
and now we're gonna start shifting from the error structure

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:19.100
in, or sorry, from the mean structure

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:20.380
into the error structure.

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:23.340
And the errors are how the individual points

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:24.820
are related to each other.

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:26.900
And so we'll know that, I mean,

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:29.340
looking at time series data,

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:31.380
usually the day before predicts the day after.

00:29:31.380 --> 00:29:34.540
So if it's 80 degrees today, or, oh, sorry, in centigrade,

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:37.420
if it's 16 today, it's gonna be,

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:39.380
the chances of it being 25 tomorrow

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:41.740
are lower than the chances of it being 18 tomorrow.

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:45.780
So if you can incorporate that into your model,

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:50.140
you're gonna end up with more accurate error values.

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:55.140
And that tells you how surprised you'll be

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:57.940
at seeing a value more extreme.

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:00.820
And so we're gonna do that,

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:03.220
and next week is the error structure,

00:30:03.220 --> 00:30:04.820
and how do we do that?

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:07.340
And so this week was all the curve fitting stuff.

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:10.940
So I will see you in the next lecture.
